  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1222 AM EST Tue Nov 10 2026\n\nPleasant fall weather will continue through Wednesday as broad \nsurface high pressure remains over the region under WNW flow aloft. \nAfter chilly early mornings, we will see mild afternoons with high \ntemperatures in the 60s for most valley locations each day through \nWednesday.  The first in a series of short waves will be approaching \nWednesday so clouds may begin to increase especially in the \nafternoon, but right now it appears it will be too dry for \nsignificant precipitation to make it to the surface on Wednesday \nalthough a light shower or sprinkle cannot be ruled out. \n\nModels continue to show poor consistency and agreement in the \ndetails for the latter part of the week, but there is good overall \nagreement and high forecast confidence that we will see a shift to \nmore winter-like conditions. The aforementioned initial short wave \nwill move across our area Wednesday night into Thursday as a deep \nupper trough develops over the eastern CONUS. A cold front will \nsweep across our area Wednesday night. We will see showers move into \nour area by Wednesday night and continue into Thursday. Convective \nenergy still looks very limited, but a few rumbles of thunder cannot \nbe ruled out.  \n\nColder air will move in behind the front, with the cold advection \nand northwest flow continuing into Saturday. Models continue to \nstruggle with the evolution and timing of the upper low that cuts \noff and moves south and southeast Friday/Friday night before lifting \noff to the northeast.  Placement of this low and the strength/timing \nof the lobes of vort energy that rotate around it will matter for \nour sensible weather, thus there is still  uncertainty on the \ndetails. However, this set up continues to look favorable for some \nsnow showers especially across the higher terrain at times in the \nThursday night through Saturday time frame, with light snow \naccumulations looking likely for the higher elevations as the snow \nshowers are enhanced by upslope flow. A flurry or light snow shower \nmay at least briefly mix in over some valley locations especially \nacross the northern half of the area. Temperatures will likely \nstruggle to make it above the 40s in most valley locations both \nFriday and Saturday.\n\nSunday and Monday look to be dry as the upper low pulls away. \nTemperatures will still be below normal but will be gradually \ntrending warmer.",
